00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.247
Bigtable is a high
performance wide column store.

00:00:54.247 --> 00:00:55.830
You can think of it
very, very roughly

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.800
as a key value store
that's distributed

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.560
across multiple machines, and
is very, very high performance

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.540
and parallelizable.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.760
Technically, it's a
sparse distributed

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.890
multi-dimensional sorted map.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.000
Bigtable is a
non-relational database.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.550
Meaning that there's no joins.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.490
It's not like a SQL database,
and it allows for easy scaling

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.620
by increasing the number
of nodes in the cluster.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.328
That's essentially
the only dial that you

00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:20.950
get to turn with Bigtable,
is the size of your cluster.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:22.470
It's scalable to petabytes,
and indeed, we've

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.887
scaled it to
petabytes at Spotify.

00:01:23.887 --> 00:01:26.370
It uses wide columns, and
again, it's non-relational.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.070
So it's not like your MySQL
or your Postgres-styles

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.722
of databases.

00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:33.180
And like I mentioned, we use
