00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:40.490
If you're not familiar
with Cloud Bigtable itself,

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.500
it allows for very
expressive schema designs.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.020
So I mentioned before
that it's non-relational,

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.867
and the schema that it gives
to you is something like this.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.700
You can have a row-key,
and all of your data

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.270
is indexed by this single key.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.953
So you don't have multiple
indices or different ways

00:02:53.953 --> 00:02:54.620
to cut the data.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.440
You essentially have a key
that you can use for lookups,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.480
and then a value
that is this kind

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.630
of multi-dimensional
object, here.

00:03:02.630 --> 00:03:04.970
You know multiple column
families within that row,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.280
multiple columns within each
of those column families,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.810
and multiple values
within each cell as well,

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.870
and those values are
all sorted by timestamp.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:13.730
So you can even do
versioning in here,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.295
and have some sort of expiration
of these values as well,

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:18.920
and this is all provided to you
by the very expressive Bigtable

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.027
API.

00:03:21.027 --> 00:03:23.360
And I don't want to get too
much into how Bigtable works

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.210
under the hood because I'm
Spotify-er, not a Googler,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.880
but Bigtable-- one very
important thing about how it

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.530
works and how it
sorts its data is

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.480
that the nodes in
your Bigtable cluster

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:34.340
are compute only, essentially.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.920
So while you might be able to
increase the number of nodes

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.252
that serve your cluster,
that gives you more capacity,

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:41.210
but it doesn't change
where the data is stored.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:43.490
The data in your cluster
is stored underlying

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:45.710
in the Colossus
storage layer, there,

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:48.140
which is a Google technology
that is not directly exposed

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.178
in Google Cloud.

00:03:49.178 --> 00:03:51.470
This means that if you want
more capacity-- if you want

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:54.770
to add more requests per
second into your system,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:56.930
you can add more
nodes, and this simply

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.090
reshards the data
across your nodes,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:02.055
and changes which data each
node is responsible for.

00:04:02.055 --> 00:04:04.430
So this doesn't actually change
where the data is stored,

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.530
it just changes which nodes
are responsible for which data.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:08.280
And this will come up
later on in the talk

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.620
about how we can scale
Bigtable very effectively.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.500
Sp I'm going to talk
quickly about three

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.840
different common Cloud
Bigtable usage patterns

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.492
that we've seen in our usage
of Bigtable at Spotify.

00:04:18.492 --> 00:04:19.950
We have lots of
different products,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.720
as I mentioned
earlier, and they all

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.242
use some variance of these
patterns that we've seen.

00:04:25.242 --> 00:04:27.450
The first pattern that we
see is using Cloud Bigtable

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.040
as a real time data store.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.680
So using it to serve some data
in real time that might be

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.930
computed offline or in batch.

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.490
The second pattern
that we've seen

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:38.070
is using Cloud Bigtable as a
caching layer, which allows us

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.500
to-- we'll essentially use
it as a large distributed

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:41.795
automatic cache.

00:04:41.795 --> 00:04:43.170
And the third
pattern is using it

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:45.492
as a platform data store,
which takes a little bit

00:04:45.492 --> 00:04:46.200
more explanation.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.440
So I'll wait until I get
there to talk about that.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.540
So first off, if we use Bigtable
as a real time data store,

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.800
Bigtable allows us to
bridge two different worlds

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.360
of data processing.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.330
We can do big batch offline
data processing that

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.760
takes lots of
resources, lots of time,

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.470
and lots of different
machines, and then

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.140
dump the results of that data
processing into Cloud Bigtable

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:08.430
to serve it in real time.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.900
This allows us to get
very low latency reads,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.587
and kind of write
this data all at once,

00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:15.170
and then read it multiple times
when different clients request

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:16.280
this data.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.770
This allows Bigtable to kind
of bridge these two worlds,

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:20.480
and do something we
couldn't have done before.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.010
You can't do this kind of
thing if you're reading off

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.650
of Google Cloud Storage, or
many other kinds of databases,

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:28.325
but Bigtable really handles
this load very, very well.

00:05:28.325 --> 00:05:30.450
The input data for a lot
of our different products,

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:33.920
for example, let's say Discover
Weekly, might come in batch.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.590
We might train a model or do
some sort of recommendation

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:39.680
product in Cloud Dataflow,
which is Google's batch

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.540
data processing framework.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.760
And then the results
of that computation

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.010
end up being stored in
Bigtable by simply writing

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.260
a huge amount of data
from Dataflow Workers

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:50.180
into a Bigtable
cluster directly,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.370
and the data sizes depend
on the application here,

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.950
but typically we tend to
use gigabytes to terabytes

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.075
for the Bigtable size here.

00:05:57.075 --> 00:05:58.700
This works really
great for write once,

00:05:58.700 --> 00:05:59.965
read many use cases.

00:05:59.965 --> 00:06:01.340
So if you have a
lot of data that

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.170
gets written once or
written infrequently,

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.587
let's say, again,
Discover Weekly.

00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:07.190
If you write that once a week,
and we want to read it multiple

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.020
times-- when users come back
to it and keep listening

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.095
to the same playlist--

00:06:10.095 --> 00:06:11.970
Bigtable works really,
really great for this.

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.640
And there's some other
great features of Bigtable

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.680
that allow this to be
even more maintenance

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.740
free and effortless, but I'll
talk about that in a second.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.740
Another use case that we
have for Cloud Bigtable

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.420
is to use it as a caching layer.

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.310
So rather than just
using the server data

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.340
in real time, which it still is
in this graph here, sometimes

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:32.300
we'll need Bigtable to cache
data in a kind of distributed

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.880
way so that systems
that are stateless

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.860
can still use this
distributed cache to avoid

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.302
having negative side
effects on other systems.

00:06:39.302 --> 00:06:40.760
So a good example
here is if we use

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.260
Cloud Dataflow in
streaming mode,

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.820
rather than in batch mode.

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:46.220
Cloud Dataflow can be used
to process streams of data.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:47.595
So as more data
comes in, you can

00:06:47.595 --> 00:06:50.120
operate the same transforms
and the same operations

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.420
on new data.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.472
And in this case, sometimes
we have Cloud Dataflow streams

00:06:54.472 --> 00:06:56.180
that need to reach
out to other services.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:58.430
They might need to talk to
back end services, or APIs,

00:06:58.430 --> 00:06:59.940
or things like that.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.815
And if we did so without
any sort of caching,

00:07:01.815 --> 00:07:03.815
this could very quickly
overwhelm these back end

00:07:03.815 --> 00:07:04.370
services.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.078
We could have a stream
of data coming in,

00:07:06.078 --> 00:07:08.480
and if we can imagine every
single element in that stream

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.670
results in a request
to a back end service,

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.655
it's very easy to overload
that, and make that fall over.

00:07:13.655 --> 00:07:15.530
Cloud Bigtable works
really, really well here

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:19.340
because we can make a request
to Bigtable as a cache first,

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.380
and we run the risk of otherwise
breaking these back end

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:22.880
services.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:25.490
So this distributive cache
adds this stateful layer

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:28.228
around the stateless
Cloud Dataflow service.

00:07:28.228 --> 00:07:30.020
Again, in this application,
the cache sizes

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.430
range from gigabytes
to terabytes,

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.347
but it really depends
on the exact application

00:07:33.347 --> 00:07:35.890
being used here.

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:38.970
All right, so our third use
case for Cloud Bigtable here,

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.770
this is a common one
that my team works on.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:43.690
This is Cloud Bigtable
as a platform data store,

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.820
and this takes a little
bit more explanation here.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.430
So we often train
multiple different types

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.013
of machine learning
models, especially

00:07:49.013 --> 00:07:50.543
for music
recommendation purposes,

00:07:50.543 --> 00:07:52.710
and these models have
slightly different properties,

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.410
or they're different versions
of the same kind of thing.

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.150
When we train these
models, we want

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.858
to produce some sort
of intermediate data

00:07:58.858 --> 00:08:00.780
that we can use
for recommendation.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.400
An example of this
is we might embed

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.110
some data into a
certain space, producing

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.290
vectors in that space.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.135
And when we take all
this resulting data,

00:08:08.135 --> 00:08:09.510
it doesn't make
sense on its own.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.010
We couldn't serve
this data directly

00:08:11.010 --> 00:08:12.908
to clients or to
users, and say here,

00:08:12.908 --> 00:08:14.200
these are your recommendations.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.520
These are not recommendations.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:16.680
These are really
intermediate pieces

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.960
of data that can be used
to produce recommendations,

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.470
but they themselves are
not the recommendations.

00:08:21.470 --> 00:08:23.130
So what we use
Bigtable for here is

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:24.930
as a platform data
store to store

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:26.705
all of this intermediate
data for all

00:08:26.705 --> 00:08:28.830
of these different models
that we might be testing,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:30.882
or might be using for
different use cases.

00:08:30.882 --> 00:08:32.340
Bigtable works
really, really great

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.600
here because we can scale
this as big as we need to.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.200
We can add more models
for experimentation.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.270
We can have bigger
models, smaller models.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.090
All this kind of stuff just
gets shoved into Bigtable,

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:44.670
and then we wrap this
Bigtable in a companion back

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:45.490
end service.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.730
So the screen box, right here.

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.860
That companion back end
service can manage things

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:51.360
like access control,
business logic,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:53.400
and caching so that
any consumers that

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.320
want to use this
recommendations data

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.560
can call the back end service,
and have one unified API

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.320
for accessing this Bigtable
that stores all the underlying

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:03.350
machine learning data.

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:06.120
So this has been a very, very
successful project of ours

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.620
to use Bigtable in
this manner, and this

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.610
does drive a lot of our
recommendation systems,

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.860
including some of the stuff
that I showed you earlier,

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.480
like the homepage on Spotify.

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.910
I mentioned a couple of
times that we do dump data

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:21.300
into Dataflow-- sorry, into
Bigtable from Cloud Dataflow,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.860
and that can be as easy as
these 10 lines of code here.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:25.890
So we have a framework
in-house and also

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.050
open source called Scio.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.960
From the Latin "to learn,"
apparently, and Scio

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.880
allows you to write very
small amounts of code

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:35.790
in Scala, in what looks
like almost like a DSL,

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:37.620
to read data from
certain places,

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.410
map it, filter it, transform
it, and essentially build up

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:44.190
an Apache Beam style
processing graph in Scala.

00:09:44.190 --> 00:09:45.810
With these 10 lines
of code here, we

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.270
can read data from a
Google Cloud Storage bucket

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:51.207
here, map that onto what we want
it to look like in Bigtable,

00:09:51.207 --> 00:09:53.040
and then with one single
line at the bottom,

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:54.480
save as Bigtable there.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:57.750
We can dump all that data
into Bigtable all at once.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:00.893
So Scio is really, really useful
for bridging these two worlds,

00:10:00.893 --> 00:10:02.310
and doing complex
data processing,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.217
and it integrates super
nicely with Bigtable.

00:10:05.217 --> 00:10:07.550
I'll talk a little bit more
about Scio later on as well,

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.383
but definitely take a
look at this on GitHub

00:10:09.383 --> 00:10:11.090
if you're interested.

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.590
I also mentioned that Bigtable
has some very nice properties

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.180
that make it good as a cache.

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.180
One of those properties is using
its built-in garbage collection

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.770
policy, or it's built-in
expiration feature.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:23.718
Now, Bigtable allows
you to set a maximum age

00:10:23.718 --> 00:10:26.010
for your data, based on the
column family and the table

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.270
that it lives in.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.700
This is directly from Google's
developer documentation,

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.010
but this max age
parameter says that maybe

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.118
after seven days or 30
days, you want the data

00:10:34.118 --> 00:10:35.910
that you've written to
automatically expire

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:36.910
from the cluster.

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:37.740
And this means
that you don't need

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.670
to do any sort of
management of your cluster.

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:41.790
You don't need to have another
process read over the data

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.380
and automatically
delete it for you.

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:45.900
This happens in the
background, opportunistically,

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:48.690
and you don't need to waste any
CPU on it, or any of your mind,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:49.320
really.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:50.950
This just happens automatically.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.700
This is super, super useful, and
you can think of many use cases

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:56.830
where if you have a write
once, read many application,

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.690
this can just make your
maintenance headache go away.

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:02.700
So I would definitely
recommend this.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.950
Another thing about using
Bigtable for these use cases

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:08.178
is that although it does have a
very expressive schema design,

00:11:08.178 --> 00:11:10.470
it's possible for you to use
all of its column families

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.450
and other features
like that to express

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.175
your data in its
language, we found

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:16.800
that it can be useful
to treat Bigtable

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:19.255
as a very, very simple map.

00:11:19.255 --> 00:11:20.880
So essentially, a
distributed key value

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.130
store where you have one
key, and you have one value.

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:25.680
So in this example,
we've kind of

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.500
ignored the column qualifier
and the column family as well.

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:30.083
We just have them
as single characters

00:11:30.083 --> 00:11:31.890
here, because honestly,
all we really need

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:35.383
is one, big distributed
key value story.

00:11:35.383 --> 00:11:37.800
And if we have these column
qualifiers and column families

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.005
as single characters, that even
makes our processing faster.

00:11:41.005 --> 00:11:42.630
We have to send those
column qualifiers

00:11:42.630 --> 00:11:44.080
on every single request.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:47.290
So keeping those as small as
possible is of interest to us.

00:11:47.290 --> 00:11:50.040
And we found that if we encode
our values as part of our JSON,

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.290
as long as we don't need to do
any filtering on those values,

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:54.930
or ask Bigtable to process them
before it fetches them for us,

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:56.910
this can be extremely
efficient, and also lends itself

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:59.070
well to doing caching in
the application layer,

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.557
and other similar things.

00:12:00.557 --> 00:12:02.640
So Bigtable, while it does
have a lot of features,

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.770
can be used in this
extremely simplistic way

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:08.430
to get really, really great
performance out of it.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.350
All right, so let's
talk a little bit

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.120
about scaling challenges.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:13.800
As I mentioned before,
we have petabytes

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.268
of data stored across
hundreds of Bigtable clusters,

00:12:16.268 --> 00:12:18.810
and so we've run into a number
of different challenges trying

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:23.022
to scale Bigtable to meet
our application load.

00:12:23.022 --> 00:12:24.480
So the first problem
we've run into

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.880
is how to scale the
cluster during ingestion.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.530
If, as I mentioned
before, we're trying

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:31.320
to ingest huge amounts of
data via Cloud Dataflow

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.510
all at once into a
Bigtable cluster,

00:12:33.510 --> 00:12:35.910
sometimes we'll easily
overrun the cluster

00:12:35.910 --> 00:12:37.840
with that write volume.

00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:38.748
This is a graph of--

00:12:38.748 --> 00:12:40.665
actually, I don't know
which cluster this was,

00:12:40.665 --> 00:12:42.630
but you can see that
at different times,

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:45.150
when we're ingesting data,
we'll have huge spikes in CV

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.970
utilization, and this is not
conducive to having a good user

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:48.712
experience.

00:12:48.712 --> 00:12:50.670
You want to make sure
that when you ingest data

00:12:50.670 --> 00:12:52.410
into your system,
you're not overloading

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:55.080
it such that the read requests
essentially get drowned out,

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.880
and have a forward latency.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.380
So what we found
is a good technique

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.172
is that we'll scale
the Bigtable cluster up

00:13:01.172 --> 00:13:02.490
just before doing ingestion.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:04.685
So we'll start our
Dataflow pipeline,

00:13:04.685 --> 00:13:06.060
and the first
thing it will do is

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:07.740
it will change the number
of nodes in the cluster

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.907
that it wants to write into.

00:13:08.907 --> 00:13:12.240
So you can do this easily
via the Bigtable API,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.430
and you'll temporarily
scale this up

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.970
by a factor of
maybe two or three,

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:17.970
or some factor that we've
empirically determined

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.345
makes sense for this use case.

00:13:20.345 --> 00:13:22.470
Once we scale it up, we'll
then ingest all the data

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:25.570
at the maximum speed that
Dataflow will allow us to do,

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:28.580
and even at that speed, we
won't impact the read requests.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.330
So we'll have still a
good user experience

00:13:30.330 --> 00:13:31.642
during that entire process.

00:13:31.642 --> 00:13:33.600
And once that's done,
we'll wait a little while

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:36.450
as well for the data to settle
down and find its right home.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.688
Then once that's done,
we can drop the size back

00:13:38.688 --> 00:13:40.980
down again. so we're not over
provisioned, and spending

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.800
extra money on capacity that
we don't need at all times.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.170
So in a timeline, this
is what this looks like.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:48.450
We add nodes to the cluster.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.710
We'll wait for the
cluster size to increase.

00:13:50.710 --> 00:13:53.180
Wait for some additional
time for caches to warm,

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:54.840
and things like that.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:57.200
We'll ingest data all at the
maximum speed we possibly

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.233
can, and then wait for
that data to settle.

00:13:59.233 --> 00:14:01.400
And once that's done, we'll
remove those extra nodes

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:03.290
at the cluster to
keep our costs low,

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:06.430
and this solution tends
to work fairly well.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:09.450
However, there is a new feature
from the Cloud Bigtables team

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.720
that might make this
no longer necessary,

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:14.310
and that is the
Bigtable replication

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:16.122
feature that they've
recently launched.

00:14:16.122 --> 00:14:18.330
Now it's possible to have
multiple clusters replicate

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:18.930
to each other.

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:21.953
Or rather, replicate from one
cluster to another cluster.

00:14:21.953 --> 00:14:23.370
And what we've
found is that if we

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:25.230
have a write cluster
and a read cluster,

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:27.010
instead of replication
in between them,

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.052
this can help us solve
this problem in a slightly

00:14:29.052 --> 00:14:30.118
different way.

00:14:30.118 --> 00:14:31.660
So in this diagram
here, at very top,

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:33.993
we have Cloud Dataflow writing
a huge amount of data in.

00:14:33.993 --> 00:14:35.670
That's that very thick arrow.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:39.000
And the write CBT cluster here
is taking all those writes in.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.780
It might be a cluster that's
larger or maybe provisioned

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:45.330
differently so that it can
accept all this capacity.

00:14:45.330 --> 00:14:47.520
And then Bigtables
asynchronous application

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:49.410
will take that data
from the write cluster,

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:51.870
and write it into the read
cluster automatically.

00:14:51.870 --> 00:14:54.450
This writing happens
in the background.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.830
It's not something you
need to manage yourself.

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.490
You just turn it on, and
then it runs when it can.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:02.080
This also means that this
usage is eventually consistent.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:04.382
So if you need instant
consistency for your data,

00:15:04.382 --> 00:15:06.090
this is not something
that you can really

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:08.910
use for that use case.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.720
But once the data
has all been adjusted

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:12.480
into the write
cluster, at some time

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.063
afterwards, that data will have
propagated through to the read

00:15:15.063 --> 00:15:17.010
cluster without effecting
the read clusters

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.222
read latency or
anything like that.

00:15:19.222 --> 00:15:19.930
And that's great.

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:21.870
That means that we can
get this high performance,

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:23.190
and the back end servers
never really know

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:24.690
that we did an
ingestion, and all

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.148
we had to do was
set up replication

00:15:26.148 --> 00:15:27.492
between two different clusters.

00:15:27.492 --> 00:15:29.950
Keep in mind, though, that if
you use a solution like this,

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:31.680
you'll have to keep
two clusters up,

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:32.790
and doing all that
management does

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:35.165
take a little bit of extra
cost, and a bit of extra time.

00:15:35.165 --> 00:15:38.040
So really, pick one of
these two solutions,

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:41.218
depending on your use case.

00:15:41.218 --> 00:15:43.510
Another problem we've had
when trying to scale Bigtable

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:45.590
involves optimizing
our key spaces.

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:47.320
So this graph on
the screen here is

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:48.970
a screenshot from
Google Stackdriver

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:50.980
tool, which allows
you to visualize

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:53.710
the activity in your
Bigtable cluster per row.

00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:56.050
So the axes here are a
little bit confusing.

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.680
Time is across the bottom.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:59.950
So from left to right, you
have time going forward.

00:15:59.950 --> 00:16:03.560
The row key in your database
is from top to bottom.

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:07.030
So you essentially have rows
that start with A at the top,

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:10.550
and rows that start with Z
at the bottom, let's say.

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:12.200
And then the color
of each cell here

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:15.720
is the activity for
that row at that time.

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:17.390
So in this graph
here, the brightness

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:18.807
on the left-hand
side of the graph

00:16:18.807 --> 00:16:20.680
is an ingestion
taking place, and then

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:22.908
over time, as the
ingestion finishes,

00:16:22.908 --> 00:16:24.950
the colors become more
muted to show that there's

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.550
less traffic over time.

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.150
But nonetheless, the traffic
is kind of evenly distributed.

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.033
No one row in this
database has more

00:16:31.033 --> 00:16:33.200
reads or writes than any
other row in this database,

00:16:33.200 --> 00:16:34.650
for instance.

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:37.070
So this is a very
important property.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:39.230
If some of these rows
received a lot more traffic

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:42.660
than other rows, this database
would not be easily scalable,

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:45.530
and I'll talk about
why in a second.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:47.590
Let's say we have a
key visualizer that

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:48.460
looks like this.

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:50.500
Very similar to the last
one, but now there's

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:53.410
a bright line across
the middle of the graph.

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.350
That bright line is telling
us that one row key has

00:16:56.350 --> 00:16:58.910
more reads and writes than
the rest of the cluster,

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:03.490
and that's a sign that this
one key, because it's so hot,

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:06.400
it's kind of limiting the
scalability of our cluster,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.990
and causing us to kind of
waste money on our cluster

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:12.220
if we try to scale it up,
and let me show why that is.

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.569
So we're going to do
an exercise together.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:15.819
You don't have to do anything.

00:17:15.819 --> 00:17:16.990
Just sit there.

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:20.410
But imagine this is
our row key space.

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:22.640
This is all of the keys
in our Bigtable cluster.

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:24.190
We have the entire
alphabet, and we

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:27.130
want this Bigtable cluster to
have two different nodes in it.

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:30.230
So a two-node
cluster with 26 keys.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:34.540
The first 13 go to node 1, and
the second 13 go to node 2.

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:36.550
This is not something
that we can configure.

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:40.240
This is how Bigtable segments
your key space, no matter what.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:41.800
So essentially,
the keys are sorted

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:44.310
alphabetically, or
technically, lexicographically.

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.060
And this means that
if you add more nodes,

00:17:46.060 --> 00:17:49.387
it'll just chop up the key
space in the same exact way,

00:17:49.387 --> 00:17:51.220
and each node will get
a proportional amount

00:17:51.220 --> 00:17:52.190
of the key space.

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:53.650
Now, there's a
big asterisk there

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:56.290
in that certain situations
can cause Bigtable

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:58.810
to kind of rebalance the
cluster a little bit more,

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:01.090
but you always have
this alphabetical setup

00:18:01.090 --> 00:18:03.080
happening here.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:05.200
So in this cluster,
node 1 and node 2

00:18:05.200 --> 00:18:07.310
both have 50% of the
traffic to the cluster.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:08.860
So the key space
is perfectly even.

00:18:08.860 --> 00:18:11.380
No key is receiving more
traffic than any other key,

00:18:11.380 --> 00:18:12.610
and it's easy to scale this.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:14.890
If we add more
nodes, we reshard,

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:16.930
and everything
gets really quick.

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:19.150
And even better, our
99th percentile read time

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:21.230
right now is only
10 milliseconds.

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:23.373
So if this is a metric
that's important,

00:18:23.373 --> 00:18:24.790
it's certainly
important to us, we

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:26.720
want to make sure that
in the 99th percentile,

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:27.970
our read times are still good.

00:18:27.970 --> 00:18:30.040
So 10 milliseconds, pretty
good for this cluster.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:32.190
That's kind of our target.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:35.290
Now, let's say that
something goes wrong.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:38.640
We get a traffic spike to a
hotkey that starts with J,

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:41.440
and J is currently
on node 1 there.

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:43.200
So with a huge
amount of new traffic

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.110
coming into this one key, what's
going to happen to the cluster?

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:47.527
And more importantly,
what's going

00:18:47.527 --> 00:18:49.050
to happen to our read time?

00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:51.360
If we do the math, it
turns out that node 1 now

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.250
gets 90% of the
traffic to the cluster,

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:54.875
and node 2 only gets 10%.

00:18:54.875 --> 00:18:56.250
Keep in mind that
node 2 is still

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.460
serving the same number
of requests it was before.

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:00.960
It's just that there's so much
more traffic going to node J,

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.252
that it's kind of skewing
the balance between these two

00:19:03.252 --> 00:19:03.795
nodes.

00:19:03.795 --> 00:19:05.670
And if we look at the
number at the top here,

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.350
the 99th percentile
read time has now

00:19:07.350 --> 00:19:09.330
shot up to 60 milliseconds.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:10.080
That's pretty bad.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:11.635
If we started at 10
milliseconds, now

00:19:11.635 --> 00:19:14.010
we're at 60, that's troubling
to trip some alarm and wake

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.880
someone up at 3:00
in the morning.

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:17.760
So if I'm that
engineer who woke up

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:20.218
at 3:00 in the morning who is
trying to solve this problem,

00:19:20.218 --> 00:19:20.880
what do I do?

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:23.910
Well, Bigtable allows me to
have one parameter to configure,

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:26.080
and that is the number
of nodes in the cluster.

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:28.680
So let's say I'm kind of groggy,
I don't know what's going on.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.263
I try to fix the
problem by turning up

00:19:30.263 --> 00:19:32.832
the number of nodes
in the cluster,

00:19:32.832 --> 00:19:33.790
and that gives me this.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:35.990
A cluster that has
four nodes in it,

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:39.550
and each of these four nodes now
has a quarter of the row space.

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:43.090
Node 1 contains A through
G, node 2 is G through M,

00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:45.620
node 3 is M through
T, and so on.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:47.698
But the key that
starts with J there

00:19:47.698 --> 00:19:49.240
is still receiving
a lot more traffic

00:19:49.240 --> 00:19:51.440
than all of the other
keys in the cluster.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.010
So if we do the
math again here, we

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:57.550
see that we now have a
very unbalanced cluster.

00:19:57.550 --> 00:19:59.200
Nodes 1, 3, and 4
are each serving

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.085
5% of traffic, which is great.

00:20:01.085 --> 00:20:03.710
They have a lot of capacity, and
they're going to be very fast.

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:06.127
However, in the worst case,
or even in the 99th percentile

00:20:06.127 --> 00:20:10.200
case, node 2 is serving
85% of our traffic,

00:20:10.200 --> 00:20:12.970
and that 85% is much worse
than we expected it to be.

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:14.680
We wanted to double
the number of nodes,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:16.810
and thus, cut the
latency in half.

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.150
But in effect, we've really
only cut the latency down

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:20.650
by three milliseconds.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:21.440
What gives?

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.482
Why is it that we can
double the number of nodes,

00:20:23.482 --> 00:20:26.020
and thus our cost, and not
get a performance gain?

00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:29.140
Well, we did the math,
and pull out your phones.

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:31.600
You might want to take
a screenshot of this.

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:34.270
This is the relationship between
the latency of your cluster

00:20:34.270 --> 00:20:38.770
and the ratio of the traffic to
your hottest key to the traffic

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.108
of the median key.

00:20:40.108 --> 00:20:42.400
To the best of my knowledge,
this is what we go off of,

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:45.070
and this is how we understand
how to scale Bigtable.

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:47.445
So if the traffic to your
hottest key is very, very high,

00:20:47.445 --> 00:20:49.653
and the traffic to your
median key is very, very low,

00:20:49.653 --> 00:20:51.700
and the ratio between
them is very significantly

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:54.880
more than one, then this is
going to limit scalability

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:55.893
by a large factor.

00:20:55.893 --> 00:20:58.060
If the traffic to the hottest
key and the median key

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:01.120
are the same, that ratio
ends up being just one,

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:02.710
and then the traffic
to your cluster

00:21:02.710 --> 00:21:05.050
is divided by all the nodes in
the cluster, and you're golden.

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:06.425
You can scale as
much as you want

00:21:06.425 --> 00:21:08.312
to just by increasing
the number of nodes.

00:21:08.312 --> 00:21:10.270
It might be more useful
to see this as a graph,

00:21:10.270 --> 00:21:13.450
though, because certainly this
doesn't make any sense to me.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:15.730
Here's this plotted as a graph.

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:17.230
So let me explain
what this means.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:18.730
Across the very
bottom of the graph,

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:21.520
here, we have the number of
nodes in your Bigtable cluster.

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:22.660
At the very left-hand
side of the graph,

00:21:22.660 --> 00:21:23.770
that's a Bigtable
cluster that has

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:26.062
one node in it, and at the
very right-hand side, that's

00:21:26.062 --> 00:21:28.030
a Bigtable cluster that
has 100 nodes in it.

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:30.340
So you can turn that
dial from 1 to 100

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:32.477
or beyond very, very easily.

00:21:32.477 --> 00:21:34.060
The four different
lines in this graph

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:35.860
represent four
different distributions

00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:37.490
of data in the cluster.

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:39.430
So the very top,
we have a red line.

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:40.930
That red line is
what happens if you

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:43.780
have one key receiving
100% of the entire traffic

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:45.040
to the cluster.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:47.080
If you add more
nodes to the cluster,

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:49.340
that will not fix your latency.

00:21:49.340 --> 00:21:51.370
It will not add throughput
because, essentially,

00:21:51.370 --> 00:21:53.745
that one key is still only
being served by one node.

00:21:53.745 --> 00:21:55.120
The additional
nodes that you add

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:56.620
don't do anything
because they don't

00:21:56.620 --> 00:21:59.210
own any data that's actually
being used for anything.

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:00.680
So that's the worst
case scenario.

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:03.520
This is why you don't want
to use Bigtable for just one

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:05.125
piece of data, essentially.

00:22:05.125 --> 00:22:07.000
The best case scenario
is the opposite, here.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:08.720
It's the green line
the very bottom.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:11.860
That's what happens if you
have flat traffic distribution.

00:22:11.860 --> 00:22:16.710
No key is more loaded than
any other key in the cluster.

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:17.990
And that green line is great.

00:22:17.990 --> 00:22:19.600
That means that if
you have one node,

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:21.442
your latency might
be 100 milliseconds.

00:22:21.442 --> 00:22:22.900
If you have two
nodes, your latency

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:24.250
might be 50 milliseconds.

00:22:24.250 --> 00:22:26.680
Four nodes, 25
milliseconds, and so on.

00:22:26.680 --> 00:22:29.590
Your latency is, essentially,
the traffic to your cluster

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:31.510
divided by the number
of nodes, and that's

00:22:31.510 --> 00:22:34.810
why we get this nice
line here because at 100

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:37.000
nodes, in theory,
each node is only

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:38.510
processing 1% of the traffic.

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:41.725
So you get a really, really
great scalability curve here.

00:22:41.725 --> 00:22:43.600
We have two curves in
the middle that kind of

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:45.760
show why you care
about this if you

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.070
have different
distributions of data.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:50.650
If one key receives 100
times the median traffic,

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:52.887
that's the orange line,
second to the top there.

00:22:52.887 --> 00:22:54.470
You can add more
nodes to the cluster,

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:55.720
and it will get better.

00:22:55.720 --> 00:22:58.750
The scalability will improve and
your p 99 will improve, but not

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:00.940
by that much, and that's
because one node is still

00:23:00.940 --> 00:23:03.670
significantly bottlenecking
the rest of the cluster.

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:05.802
The yellow line, second
from the bottom there,

00:23:05.802 --> 00:23:06.760
that's another example.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:08.260
That's what happens
if one key receives

00:23:08.260 --> 00:23:10.660
10 times the median traffic,
which doesn't seem like too

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:12.610
much, but adding more
nodes will still not

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:15.390
increase your performance
in the worst case.

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:17.140
So this is definitely
something that we've

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:19.898
used to try and re-architect
our systems at Spotify

00:23:19.898 --> 00:23:21.940
to make sure that everyone
gets a good experience

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:24.680
every single time.

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:28.250
All right, so if having your key
space designed in a certain way

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.720
is super important, how do
you design your schemas?

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.270
What are some good
tips and tricks

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:35.150
to make sure you don't
run into this problem?

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:37.490
Well, choosing an evenly
distributed key space

00:23:37.490 --> 00:23:38.990
is probably the
number one thing you

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:41.420
need to care about in your
schema design for Bigtable.

00:23:41.420 --> 00:23:44.690
And there are three
strategies I've run into here.

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:46.400
They're in order
of worst to best.

00:23:46.400 --> 00:23:48.650
So strategy number one is
not something you should do.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:49.550
Strategy number
three is something

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:50.420
that I would recommend doing.

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:51.800
So I'll go through
them in order,

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.330
and talk about why you shouldn't
do them or should do them.

00:23:54.330 --> 00:23:56.330
So the first strategy for
distributing your keys

00:23:56.330 --> 00:23:58.855
across the entire key space
is to hash all the keys.

00:23:58.855 --> 00:24:00.980
So you could take the keys
that you would regularly

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:01.790
put in the database.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:03.290
Let's say that maybe they're
user names or something

00:24:03.290 --> 00:24:04.850
like that, and just hash them.

00:24:04.850 --> 00:24:06.792
Pick a non-cryptographic
hash function.

00:24:06.792 --> 00:24:08.000
It doesn't need to be secure.

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.580
It just needs to be fast
because the only purpose of this

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:13.412
is to distribute this
data across the cluster.

00:24:13.412 --> 00:24:14.120
This works great.

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:15.830
And technically,
you can do this,

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:17.430
and you'll get good performance.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:20.570
However, this is not good for
a whole bunch of other reasons.

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:23.360
If you hash this data
randomly, or you hash it

00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.010
with a hash function, you
can't use CBT anymore.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:27.620
You can't use your
debugging tools.

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:30.947
You can't read the key
space on your screen

00:24:30.947 --> 00:24:32.780
because it doesn't
really make sense to you.

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:35.900
It's essentially obfuscated, and
that's not good for debugging

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:38.430
or for developing applications.

00:24:38.430 --> 00:24:40.460
You also can't do range
scans on this data.

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:43.460
So if your keys have
common prefixes,

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:45.650
it's very easy in Bigtable
to do range scans,

00:24:45.650 --> 00:24:48.440
and say start at this key,
and go until you hit this key.

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:50.210
Well, if your keys
are hashed, they're

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:51.710
effectively randomly
distributed.

00:24:51.710 --> 00:24:53.450
Which is great for
performance, but not

00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:55.220
good for any sort
of data locality,

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:59.670
or understanding where data
is similar to other data.

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.820
So a good second
approach is to reverse

00:25:01.820 --> 00:25:03.140
all the keys in your key space.

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:04.580
So we tried this
for a little while.

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:06.580
Assuming you have some
sort of numeric ordering,

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:09.500
meaning that the digit at
the very right of your data,

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:11.150
or at the least
significant digit

00:25:11.150 --> 00:25:13.420
changes more often than
the most significant digit.

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:15.170
So an example of this
is maybe timestamps.

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:16.910
If you had timestamps
in Bigtable,

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:19.100
you could reverse the
keys, and then the digit

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:20.918
that changes most
frequently every second

00:25:20.918 --> 00:25:22.460
would, essentially,
scatter your data

00:25:22.460 --> 00:25:24.780
across all the different
nodes in the cluster.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:26.360
This, again, technically works.

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:28.280
You could reverse all the keys
before inserting the data,

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:30.260
and then reverse all the
keys when you read again.

00:25:30.260 --> 00:25:32.200
This is also more human
readable and more debugable

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:33.440
because you can see
that data there.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:34.880
You can read it
backwards and put it

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:36.547
through a script or
something like that,

00:25:36.547 --> 00:25:38.420
and now it's
possible to do that.

00:25:38.420 --> 00:25:40.200
But this is still not great.

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.060
There's still a better
solution available,

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:43.260
and that's solution
number three, here,

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:45.680
which is to promote the most
random field from your data

00:25:45.680 --> 00:25:46.748
to the row key.

00:25:46.748 --> 00:25:48.290
So try to find
something in your data

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:52.863
that can be used to impart some
randomness into your row key,

00:25:52.863 --> 00:25:54.530
and move that from
the value that you're

00:25:54.530 --> 00:25:56.700
storing into the row itself.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:25:58.700
So if you have some sort
of a random identifier,

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.370
let's say maybe it's an
item ID or a UU idea,

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:04.550
or something like that, and if
that's consistent in your data,

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:06.763
and it's random, but
it's deterministic,

00:26:06.763 --> 00:26:08.180
try to put that
into your row key,

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:10.640
thus distributing that data
randomly across your cluster,

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:12.307
but still giving you
some business logic

00:26:12.307 --> 00:26:13.760
you can do on top of that.

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.630
This preserves readability,
uses very little CPU overhead,

00:26:16.630 --> 00:26:19.010
aids debugging, and allows
you to do range scans

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:22.530
if those keys are consistent.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:24.305
So I keep talking about
these range scans,

00:26:24.305 --> 00:26:26.430
and I haven't really defined
exactly what they are.

00:26:26.430 --> 00:26:28.640
So in Bigtable,
because all the data

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.490
is ordered alphabetically
or lexicographically,

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:34.250
you can scan from one key to
another very, very simply.

00:26:34.250 --> 00:26:36.950
You can tell Bigtable to start
a scan at a certain place,

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:38.990
and end a scan at another
place, and even do

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:40.990
a prefix scan on that
because it's, essentially,

00:26:40.990 --> 00:26:42.203
the same thing.

00:26:42.203 --> 00:26:44.120
If you have a random
distribution of your data

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.220
across the cluster
and your key space,

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.290
you can still do these
row range scans provided

00:26:48.290 --> 00:26:50.332
that you know what the
first section of your data

00:26:50.332 --> 00:26:51.420
looks like.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.980
And if you exploit this even
more, you can have tiered keys.

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:58.160
So tiered keys are where the
first section of your key

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.620
is kind of a superset of all
the later sections of the key,

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:03.270
and let me talk a little
bit of what that means.

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:04.850
So here's a very
contrived example.

00:27:04.850 --> 00:27:07.850
Don't do this, it's just for
explanation purposes here.

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:10.100
But let's say that you're
keying your data in Bigtable

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:11.267
by keys that look like this.

00:27:11.267 --> 00:27:13.370
They start with a country
that your data is in,

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:15.470
the region, the city,
and then the district,

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:17.730
and all of these
form the same key.

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:19.160
So your keys might
look something

00:27:19.160 --> 00:27:21.535
like this, where you'd have
the country at the first part

00:27:21.535 --> 00:27:24.380
here, so Canada, followed by the
region, or state, or province,

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:25.580
or whatever you have there.

00:27:25.580 --> 00:27:27.535
Then a city, and
then a district.

00:27:27.535 --> 00:27:29.910
This means that you can do
range scans on this data very,

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:30.618
very effectively.

00:27:30.618 --> 00:27:33.150
If you wanted to find all
of the cities in California,

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:34.720
let's say, you
could ask Bigtable

00:27:34.720 --> 00:27:36.720
to range scan from
the start of USA colon

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:39.890
California to the end
of USA colon California,

00:27:39.890 --> 00:27:42.980
and that would give you all
those rows in one query.

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.630
This is great,
however, this still

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:46.130
doesn't randomly
distribute the data

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:47.392
across your entire database.

00:27:47.392 --> 00:27:49.100
So you have a bit of
performance problems

00:27:49.100 --> 00:27:52.130
here, and one
strategy around that

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:54.170
is to remove the
first part of the key,

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.240
if you know that it
has low cardinality.

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:59.610
So in this case, there are only
190-odd countries in the world,

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.407
and because of that,
all of the USA data

00:28:01.407 --> 00:28:03.240
would be at the very
bottom of your cluster.

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:04.557
At those nodes there.

00:28:04.557 --> 00:28:06.140
And all the data for
certain countries

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:08.940
would be kind of right
next to each other.

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:11.060
So to get around that,
you can take this mapping

00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:12.830
of, let's say,
country to region,

00:28:12.830 --> 00:28:15.830
or some other very, very
low cardinality mapping,

00:28:15.830 --> 00:28:17.420
and put that into
your application.

00:28:17.420 --> 00:28:21.260
And then in Bigtable, you could
store just the second layer

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:22.282
of your key forward.

00:28:22.282 --> 00:28:23.990
There are many more
regions in the world,

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:26.240
many more states and provinces
than there are countries,

00:28:26.240 --> 00:28:28.640
and now doing this allows you to
randomly distribute this data--

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:30.530
not randomly, but put
that across the cluster

00:28:30.530 --> 00:28:34.580
in a way that helps you
scale without hot spotting

00:28:34.580 --> 00:28:38.100
on certain areas of the cluster.

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:40.110
Let's talk about one
more example here.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:42.740
This is a key design for
doing row range scans

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:44.550
over data that involves IDs.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:46.790
So this is a real example
from Spotify's data.

00:28:46.790 --> 00:28:50.210
Obviously, AAA1 and
BBB2 are not real users.

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:52.490
This is all kind of made
up, but this is a real task

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:54.950
that we have to solve.

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:56.870
What do we do if we
want to know what

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:58.560
tracks a user has listened to?

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:01.040
So if I'm AAA1,
and I've listened

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.790
to track one at timestamp 123,
we can store that in Bigtable

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:06.330
with a schema that
looks like this.

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:09.170
Start with AAA1, then the second
part of the key is track one,

00:29:09.170 --> 00:29:11.030
and then the timestamp
encodes exactly when

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:12.433
I listened to that.

00:29:12.433 --> 00:29:14.600
And then if I wanted to do
a range scan and find out

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:17.450
exactly when this user has
listened to all these tracks,

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.130
well, I Bigtable
to range scan AAA1.

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:22.632
Give me all the rows that
start with this value,

00:29:22.632 --> 00:29:24.590
and it gives me back
hundreds, maybe thousands,

00:29:24.590 --> 00:29:26.460
maybe tens of thousands of rows.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:28.160
And each row contains
both that user

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:29.810
ID, and the track
they've listened to,

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:32.010
and the time they
listened to that track.

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:34.580
Now, because this is a
tiered key structure,

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:35.720
I can go even further.

00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:38.960
I can say tell me not
just when user AAA1 has

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:41.750
listened to this track, but
specifically, track two.

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:46.220
So if I do a row range scan
from AAA1, including track two,

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:48.320
I'll get all the
different plays that user

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:50.060
has had of that track,
and that gives me

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:52.602
all those rows that are kind of
continuous next to each other

00:29:52.602 --> 00:29:53.970
in that Bigtable cluster.

00:29:53.970 --> 00:29:55.700
So this is a super
useful technique

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:58.550
to try and put your
data in a format that

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.250
will be both scalable and
performant across Bigtable,

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:02.750
but also in a way
that you can still

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:04.625
get this kind of
performance you'd expect out

00:30:04.625 --> 00:30:08.350
of a relational database
with multiple indices.

00:30:08.350 --> 00:30:10.250
OK, I'm just running along here.

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:12.740
This took less time than
I expected, but let's talk

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:14.060
a little bit about open source.

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:15.470
So I mentioned earlier,
we have some tools that

00:30:15.470 --> 00:30:16.620
are very, very useful.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:18.710
You can use this in
your own pipelines,

00:30:18.710 --> 00:30:21.350
and get your data
processing done much quicker

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:23.520
with some of these tools.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:24.930
So we love open source.

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:26.930
We have a lot of open
source projects on GitHub.

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:29.630
If you check out
GitHub/Spotify, you'll

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:31.703
find these projects,
and many, many more.

00:30:31.703 --> 00:30:34.370
The first project I want to talk
about is a project called Scio.

00:30:34.370 --> 00:30:36.110
So like I mentioned
before, Scio comes

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:37.550
from the Latin "to learn."

00:30:37.550 --> 00:30:41.840
It is a Scala based framework
for Apache Beam and Google

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:43.520
Cloud Dataflow,
and it's inspired

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:45.350
by Apache Spark and Scalding.

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.660
It's fairly popular in the
open source community now.

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:49.700
It's got a couple
thousand stars on GitHub,

00:30:49.700 --> 00:30:51.320
if that's the measure
of popularity,

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:52.820
and we use this
very, very heavily

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:55.920
internally to try and do
all of our data processing.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:58.010
So like the example I
showed you before, this

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:00.690
allows us to, let's
say, grab data from GCS,

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:03.320
and dump that into the
Bigtable with maybe

00:31:03.320 --> 00:31:04.980
only 10 lines of code.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.310
We could also do
complicated data pipelines.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.193
You can map, filter,
do all the operations

00:31:09.193 --> 00:31:11.360
you'd expect from a functional
programming language,

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:15.560
but across large sets of data
on Cloud Dataflow in the cloud.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:17.210
So Scio is super, super useful.

00:31:17.210 --> 00:31:18.857
Check that out on GitHub.

00:31:18.857 --> 00:31:21.440
There are lots of tutorials and
other documentation available,

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:24.320
and we'd love to see
you start using that.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.940
Simple Bigtable is the second
thing on the slide here.

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:29.420
This is a simpler, more
asynchronous Bigtable client

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:31.280
for Java and Scala.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:34.070
Now, the official Bigtable
client works just fine.

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:35.480
It's just a little bit verbose.

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:38.160
So what we did was we
wrote a simpler client.

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:39.792
It's based on the
Java builder pattern,

00:31:39.792 --> 00:31:41.750
and it allows you to do
asynchronous operations

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:44.720
against Bigtable with a
very small amount of code.

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.180
This also powers a lot of
our production experiences.

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:48.950
When I mentioned earlier that
Discover Weekly, and the Home

00:31:48.950 --> 00:31:51.380
Page, and stuff like that is
all served out of Bigtable,

00:31:51.380 --> 00:31:52.860
it's going through this library.

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:54.740
So this is production
ready code.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:57.590
Try to use that, and you'll
see your Bigtable interactions

00:31:57.590 --> 00:32:00.753
become much terser in your
code, and easier to work with.

00:32:00.753 --> 00:32:02.420
And finally, I want
to talk a little bit

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:04.970
about a project called
Annoy, and Annoy

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:08.720
is a library for approximate
nearest neighbors calculations.

00:32:08.720 --> 00:32:11.000
What that means is if you're
doing machine learning

00:32:11.000 --> 00:32:12.680
and you have
representations of data,

00:32:12.680 --> 00:32:15.868
Annoy allows you to select
similar data in spaces.

00:32:15.868 --> 00:32:17.660
I'm not doing a machine
learning talk here,

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:19.368
so I'm not going to
get too much into it,

00:32:19.368 --> 00:32:21.740
but it's approximate nearest
neighbors, and the OY

00:32:21.740 --> 00:32:23.840
I think stands for Oh Yeah.

00:32:23.840 --> 00:32:25.700
But Annoy, again, on GitHub.

00:32:25.700 --> 00:32:27.490
Very popular with lots of stars.

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:28.785
Very performant library.

00:32:28.785 --> 00:32:30.660
Not the most performant
library in the world,

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:32.210
but it has great
support bindings

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:34.640
for most major languages,
and it's very widely

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:35.820
used in the community.

00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:37.820
So try to use Annoy if
you have machine learning

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:40.563
tasks that could use
nearest neighbor lookups.

00:32:40.563 --> 00:32:42.480
And with that, I've
breezed through this talk.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:44.397
Again, my name is Peter
Sobot, Senior Engineer

00:32:44.397 --> 00:32:46.500
on the Recommendations
team at Spotify, New York.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.220
Tweet me @psobot or @SpotifyEng.

00:32:49.220 --> 00:32:52.340
And please, do give
feedback in the app there--

00:32:52.340 --> 00:32:54.290
in the Google Cloud Next app.

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:56.362
This feedback goes back
to the Google Cloud team

00:32:56.362 --> 00:32:58.320
to figure out which
